# docs/dirty-plan.md. Each backend spells its own affordance (a macOS
# close-button dot, a `*` in the Windows caption, a GTK header bullet,
# nothing on the phones) and expect_dirty asks one question of all.
# THE TITLE IS ASSERTED ONLY WHILE CLEAN: the WinUI asterisk rides the
# RENDERED caption, which is what expect_title reads there, so a title
# read inside the dirty stretch cannot hold on five lanes.
expect label#0 "notes"
expect label#1 "saved"
expect_title "dirty"
expect_dirty false
# An edit changes the model AND declares the window dirty: neither
# implies the other, since kaya does not watch your signals for you.
click button#0
expect label#0 "notes and a line"
expect label#1 "unsaved"
expect_dirty true
# Saving clears both, so the mark comes DOWN as well as up: a lowering
# that only ever sets the flag passes every assertion above.
click button#1
expect label#1 "saved"
expect_dirty false
# D3: the prop arms NOTHING. The close fires the veto class, the app
# opens its own dialog, and cancelling keeps the window with the mark
# still up — answering a dialog is not saving.
click button#0
expect label#1 "unsaved"
expect_dirty true
close_window window#0
expect_alert "unsaved changes"
alert_choose cancel
expect label#1 "kept editing"
expect_windows 1
expect_dirty true
